Skia: a translucent rectangle blends over what is drawn, on Windows as on other platforms.

On Windows, OutputDevice::DrawTransparent(tools::PolyPolygon, percent) sends a rectangle to the
emulation path, which calls DrawAlphaRect, to avoid GDI+ dithering on remote displays. Skia's
drawAlphaRect filled (and outlined) with the "source" blend mode, the default since the alpha
device was removed from OutputDevice. That stores the colour with its alpha instead of blending
it, and the screen shows the premultiplied colour: a dark box over whatever was there. Upstream
fixed the polygon path the same way (tdf#167643, tdf#169914); the rectangle path was left.

User-visible in HST Office on Windows (where 26.8 always renders with Skia):
- Calc's Text Import dialog: the header of a selected column became a near-black block,
  (23, 27, 31) for the active colour at 85% transparency, and hid the column type;
- Calc's reference marks while editing a formula (the corner squares, 40% transparency);
- ValueSet hover outlines (55%), DrawSelectionBackground for flat rectangles (header bars,
  icon views), and other rectangle DrawTransparent callers.

drawAlphaRect now uses SrcOver when the rectangle is translucent; an opaque one (drawRect) keeps
the "source" mode, and the XOR and invert modes are unchanged. The unit test draws a translucent
filled and outlined rectangle on white and checks that it blends.
tests/runtime/check-windows-transparent-rects.py checks the Text Import header on Windows.

License: modified upstream files keep their license (MPL-2.0).

